WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:04.228
(light instrumental music)

00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:06.361
- [Narrator] It's taken
6,000 years for nature

00:00:06.361 --> 00:00:09.444
to carve these remarkable formations.

00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:14.486
The marble caves of
Chile Chico are located

00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:17.050
on a peninsula of solid marble bordering

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.217
a remote glacial lake that spans
the Chile/Argentina border.

00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:25.579
The lake is fed by rivers coming

00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:28.412
from several surrounding glaciers.

00:00:29.661 --> 00:00:31.415
As the glaciers melt and grow,

00:00:31.415 --> 00:00:34.381
the waters slowly carve out the marble

00:00:34.381 --> 00:00:38.108
leaving behind masterpieces
fit for a museum.

00:00:38.108 --> 00:00:40.579
These distinct bright
blue waters are caused

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:43.091
by suspended particles from the glaciers

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:46.174
reflecting the blue part of sunlight.

00:00:47.582 --> 00:00:49.972
Getting to this location isn't easy.

00:00:49.972 --> 00:00:52.363
It's a long journey requiring a flight,

00:00:52.363 --> 00:00:54.751
a 200-mile drive into the wilderness

00:00:54.751 --> 00:00:58.910
and a boat ride just to get
there, but it's worth it.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.384
The remarkable swirling patterns on the cave

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:03.939
reflect the lake's blue waters changing

00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:07.022
with the water levels and the season.

00:01:11.629 --> 00:01:14.629
Nature truly is the greatest artist.

